#version 450
//! `activate(gate) * up` over a fused `[gate | up]` projection, in one dispatch.
//!
//! # Why this is its own shader
//!
//! A gated feed-forward was four dispatches: two `SliceChannels` copies to split the projection,
//! an `Activate` on the gate half, and a `Mul` against the up half. Three of those move data
//! only to hand it to the next one.
//!
//! That is cheap on a desktop and is not on a phone. A Gemma 4 decode step is 1,094 dispatches
//! of which only 245 carry weights; the rest are elementwise work over a few kilobytes, and each
//! costs far more in dispatch and barrier overhead than in arithmetic. Two gated feed-forwards
//! per layer across 35 layers is 210 dispatches spent on something expressible as one.
//!
//! The slices disappear entirely: the halves are contiguous, so this reads both directly by
//! offset instead of copying each into scratch first.
//!
//! # Push fields
//!
//! `p.in0` is the `[2 * inner, 1, T]` projection and `p.out_at` the `[inner, 1, T]` result.
//! `p.count` is the output's element count. `p.in_c` is `inner`, the distance from a gate element
//! to its up partner. `p.act` is the [`nets::Act`] code and `p.act_weight` the slope tensor when
//! it is `PRelu`. `p.out_h` and `p.out_w` give the spatial size, so a per-channel activation can
//! recover its channel.
#include "common.glsl"
void main() {
    uint index = global_index();
    if (index >= p.count) {
        return;
    }
    // Both halves come from one tensor, so the up element is a fixed stride from the gate one.
    // `p.in_c` is that stride in elements, which is `inner * spatial` - the whole gate half -
    // rather than `inner`, because the layout is channel-major and T may exceed one.
    uint spatial = max(p.out_h * p.out_w, 1u);
    uint channel = index / spatial;
    float gate = float(arena[p.in0 + index]);
    float up = float(arena[p.in0 + p.in_c + index]);
    arena[p.out_at + index] = float16_t(activate(gate, p.act, channel) * up);
}
